WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.480
Hello! Did you hear about Dall-E? No, not WALL-E,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.400
Dall-E. No, not the human painter,&nbsp;
the transformer-based AI painter!&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.600
Twitter exploded again because OpenAI’s new image&nbsp;
generation model, called Dall-E was announced.&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.080
So here we are, trying to figure out how&nbsp;
cool it is and how this could happen.&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.040
Image generation was already spectacular with&nbsp;
dedicated models used for rendering people.&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.520
There were also models for&nbsp;
rendering animals from textual input&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.280
but the difficulty was to generate these kinds of&nbsp;
images from text when the text combines concepts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.240
and the image has to produce plausible&nbsp;
combinations of these concepts.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.800
So OpenAI looked down upon the world, decided&nbsp;
to stop resting after publishing the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.760
GPT-3 and quickly solve the problem&nbsp;
of image generation from text too!&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.800
Yes, just like that, DALL-E was born.
And OpenAI rested the next day. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.120
no, they should not rest because they&nbsp;
still have a paper to write about Dall-E.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.440
And of course, we do not have access to the model,&nbsp;
so we only have OpenAIs blog post to look at.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.960
But the situation is not new, they did&nbsp;
the same with GPT-3. Perhaps again a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.840
multi-dollar company will buy exclusive rights&nbsp;
to the model and we will never see it again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.200
Ok, Ms. Coffee Bean, you were cynical enough.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.560
So here it is: DALL-E creates images that combine&nbsp;
unrelated concepts in plausible ways, check.&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.920
It can also generate store front text. I&nbsp;
have never seen anything like this, did you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.080
Let us know in the comments if you did.&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:57.280
DALL-E can do also style transfer by a&nbsp;
text prompt. So it can modify the style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.680
of an image following a textual description&nbsp;
of the desired output. Wow, how crazy is this?

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.400
Ok, enough showing off: what is&nbsp;
this model and how can it work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.480
DALL-E is, I cite “a 12-billion&nbsp;
parameter version of GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.000
trained to generate images from textual&nbsp;
descriptions”. Oh, that explains everything.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.280
No, it does not.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:30.320
GPT-3 looked at text, not at images, what&nbsp;
do images and text have even in common?&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.720
Ms. Coffee Bean has a variety of videos on&nbsp;
this, trying to explain that images and text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.320
are not that different kinds of input for&nbsp;
a transformer that usually works on sets.&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.560
As long as both images and text are regarded&nbsp;
somehow as sequences or elements in a set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.880
everything works again. There have been some&nbsp;
kinds of image sequentialization approaches in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.240
the literature, but it is safe to assume&nbsp;
that OpenAI stuck to their approach from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:06.240
ImageGPT, where they sequentialize&nbsp;
the image row by row into a 1d vector.&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.080
So now that we know how images can be sequences,&nbsp;
we are ready to know how DALL-E is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:19.440
GPT-3 also looking at images. DALL-E is also a&nbsp;
language model that receives both text tokens and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:28.240
sequentialised images as input, up to 1280 tokens.&nbsp;
Why is the sequence length limited to this number?&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.120
Because attention scales quadratically&nbsp;
and increasing the input size can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.280
really blow up computational time.
Then like in language modelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.760
the goal is to predict one token after the next.&nbsp;
Text tokens are words or subwords and image tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:49.760
are image pixel values. This also means that&nbsp;
DALL-E does not only generate images from text,&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.800
but can also continue images if the&nbsp;
“beginning” of the image is already in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.840
the prompt following the text. So that was if for&nbsp;
the technical details. Ok, but why does it work?

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.600
Why did the image generation problems&nbsp;
need specific GAN or VAE architecture to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.320
solve the problem only partially and now&nbsp;
a transformer-based model can do it all?

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.480
Well, because of the formidable&nbsp;
amount of training data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.000
How much data was used exactly we do not know,&nbsp;
but it must have been the whole internet, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.320
OpenAI also did with GPT-3. And things that&nbsp;
were not possible with academic sized datasets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.880
have now become feasible with&nbsp;
a model that has seen it all.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.480
This does not make the DALL-E results less&nbsp;
impressive, but the creativity that we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.880
with DALL-E has something to do with&nbsp;
the variance of the data it has seen.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.480
Take this example, where they visualize&nbsp;
perspective and three-dimensionality. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.920
perspective and 3D look very good in general,&nbsp;
but impressive for Ms. Coffee Bean is how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:03.520
model knows to make a capybara from voxels. It&nbsp;
becomes clear that something like this is possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.040
only because of all the Minecraft and other&nbsp;
pixel art images that the model had access to.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.080
We would never expect from a StyleGan to&nbsp;
make something sensible out of the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.000
voxel if it has never seen these voxelized&nbsp;
images before in the gaming or art area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:27.920
of the internet and has been limited to a&nbsp;
benchmark dataset of celebrity faces only. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:34.720
enormous data and variance, comes great power.
Let’s look at more showing off of this power:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.200
OpenAI invested a lot of time into this blog-post.&nbsp;
Did you ever have trouble imagining a cube made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:51.840
out of porcupine? Well, wait no more, here it is!
Okay, so... a cube with the texture of keyboard...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:56.400
okay.
One can also draw multiple boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:05.840
and really tell the model how to render these.
Wow, this is awesome!

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.240
Ohh, these plates are so cute!
And so smol!

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.760
Were you making a YouTube video but did not&nbsp;
have exactly that emoji you were imagining?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.800
Wait no more! DALL-E is here to paint it for you!
And this is very interesting from a multimodal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.520
perspective that one description&nbsp;
can have so many possible outcomes!&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.880
Wow, this is amazing, I can&nbsp;
play with it the whole day.&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.480
Remember the image completion example?
Well, one can even change the pose of this bust.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:46.800
Okay, here are some failure cases. Interesting to&nbsp;
see, especially because we don't know if OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.240
was cherry-picking in this blog post. We don't&nbsp;
know because we don't have access to the model.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:58.160
And of course, the applications are limit-less! Do&nbsp;
you want to design a new t-shirt? Just tell DALL-E&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:04.320
how to draw it and then you have many versions of&nbsp;
said t-shirt on a mannequin because you introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.400
the mannequin head with the image prompt and well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:16.080
you have here your designed fashion!
This has huge industrial applications! $$$

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.520
And yes, the combination of unrelated&nbsp;
concepts! Wow, I mean... this is... is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:28.400
this art? One could ask. It made a snail into a&nbsp;
harp or you know... it made a harp into a snail!

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.920
And this can be creativity, one might think.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:39.200
So it is exactly how they put it: it is a&nbsp;
3D rendering engine via natural language.&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:45.120
You just say what you want&nbsp;
DALL-E to paint and it paints it!

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.640
And you can expect the model to fill in the&nbsp;
blanks which means that you underspecify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.400
your rendering instructions and the model comes&nbsp;
up with something. And we cannot tell for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.280
but it might just be that this something&nbsp;
that it fills in the blanks with is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.840
the most probable example that&nbsp;
it has seen in the training set.&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:12.240
Which already opens the pandora box of bias.
In the blog they promise to investigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:17.120
the societal impacts and check for&nbsp;
biases. Hopefully sooner than later...&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.920
Until then or until they release the model, one&nbsp;
can just scroll through the blog indefinitely!

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.520
But we really can not deny it, these&nbsp;
are impressive results from DALL-E,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.440
bridging the gap between vision and&nbsp;
language, stepping into the multimodal realm.&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.360
Multimodality is my dearest&nbsp;
topic in Machine Learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:46.160
Ms. Coffee Bean, what is your favourite topic?
Lalali… Oh, Ok, you do not want to tell us. Fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:53.920
But back to DALL-E: However excited I might be&nbsp;
about this, I have to say, I am also a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:09:01.120
frustrated about all this, because throwing&nbsp;
the whole data of the internet at a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:07.360
solves the the problem rather than clever tricks&nbsp;
and gimmicks presented so far in the multimodality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:15.920
literature... you know, by ordinary mortals unlike&nbsp;
OpenAI. Especially as a PhD student, one has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:25.440
take these results you know... a little diluted to&nbsp;
still feel a little relevant in this whole system.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:32.080
But, you know, everything is not done yet, future&nbsp;
research will tell us about the real capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:38.160
and problems that these model brings with them.
Also I really hope that they will keep their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.760
promise and investigate the&nbsp;
societal impacts and biases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:48.000
Until then, I will keep scrolling this blog in&nbsp;
awe, Ms. Coffee Bean will get back to making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.560
another video and you know... what about you?
Let us know in the comments what *you* will do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:03.840
and hope to see you in the next video.
